WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:08.080
Star-crossed love between an Elf and a Human, a&nbsp;
wizard-like stranger, and a world-destroying oath.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:13.834
"The Rings of Power" is filled with subtle&nbsp;
details only true fans will recognize.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:14.880
Spoilers ahead!

00:00:15.334 --> 00:00:19.600
It doesn't take long for subtle references&nbsp;
to start showing up in "The Rings of Power."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:23.040
In fact, there's one that appears&nbsp;
during the show's prologue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:28.480
As Galadriel narrates about her fallen brother,&nbsp;
Finrod Felagund, we see his corpse lying on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:33.920
table under a cloth. His arms are out, and&nbsp;
his flesh is scarred with numerous wounds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:40.720
The video highlights one of these cuts&nbsp;
specifically: the mark that Sauron puts on him.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:45.760
Sauron's brand isn't canon. In the source&nbsp;
material, the Dark Lord doesn't go around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:52.000
burning a sign into people's flesh. But he does&nbsp;
leave quite a few marks on the heroic Finrod.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:57.520
In "The Silmarillion," Finrod helps the heroes&nbsp;
Beren and Lúthien in their quest to reclaim one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:03.440
the titular hallowed jewels called the Silmarils.&nbsp;
In the process, he and his companions are captured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:10.240
by Sauron. The villain, who isn't the Dark Lord&nbsp;
yet, kills off the group one at a time using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:17.120
werewolves. As one of the werewolves makes for&nbsp;
Beren, Finrod uses all of his power to defeat it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:22.560
though his wounds were mortal. Those&nbsp;
scratches aren't from a scrape in battle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:29.435
They're not from swords or spears or arrows.&nbsp;
Those are bona fide dying werewolf wounds.

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:33.520
Another Easter egg tucked into the&nbsp;
early moments of "The Rings of Power"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:37.840
appears when several Elves draw their&nbsp;
swords together in quick succession.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:43.200
For fans familiar with Tolkien's lore, the&nbsp;
scene immediately brings to mind the famous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:48.080
Oath of Fëanor from "The Silmarillion."&nbsp;
Galadriel's brother Finrod is amongst them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:53.200
which is out of place considering the source&nbsp;
material, but otherwise everything lines up.

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:58.240
The event, while skimmed over in the prologue of&nbsp;
the show, is a watershed moment in Middle-earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:04.320
history. The oath is taken by the famous&nbsp;
Elvish character Fëanor and his seven sons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:10.400
It's an irrevocable oath that leads to a lot&nbsp;
of tragedy. And if it is the Oath of Fëanor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:16.080
then Finrod shouldn't be there. But the setting&nbsp;
and actions, however briefly they're depicted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:21.840
seems to be a direct nod toward the oath that&nbsp;
is so critical to the stories that follow.

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:27.360
"The Rings of Power" doesn't have access to "The&nbsp;
Silmarillion," which is probably why its prologue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:33.120
is so quick and non-specific. It skims over many&nbsp;
of the major events that take place in that book.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:39.600
Some are easy to spot, like the Oath of Fëanor.&nbsp;
Others seem to be a murky mash-up of multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:45.680
events presented at the same time, including&nbsp;
a series of battles and violent conflicts.

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:51.040
These start with a shot of a dragon throwing a&nbsp;
giant eagle to the ground in a blaze of fire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:56.640
This is an event that could have only&nbsp;
happened during the Earth-shattering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:03.600
age-ending War of Wrath right before "The Rings&nbsp;
of Power" starts. That's the battle where Morgoth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:07.120
the first Dark Lord, is finally defeated.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:13.520
When Galadriel picks up a helmet and puts it into&nbsp;
a gigantic pile of other helmets, the scene calls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:19.760
another battle to mind. One of the most tragic&nbsp;
events of the First Age of Middle-earth history is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:26.320
called the Battle of Unnumbered Tears. After the&nbsp;
battle, which goes badly for the army of Elves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:32.080
a giant mountain of bodies is created called&nbsp;
the Hill of the Slain. While it isn't quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:36.800
the same thing as a bunch of helmets, the&nbsp;
feel of Galadriel's sorrowful experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:42.852
in the aftermath of the unnamed battlefield&nbsp;
seems to hint at that famously tragic event.

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:46.560
In Peter Jackson's extended edition&nbsp;
of "The Fellowship of the Ring"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:51.760
Galadriel sails a swan boat in Lothlórien.&nbsp;
In "The Rings of Power," we see Galadriel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:57.200
sailing to Valinor in a boat with a similar&nbsp;
bird-like prow. In the opening scene of the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:00.720
a young Galadriel also makes&nbsp;
a paper boat that doesn't just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:08.720
float, it sails. And as it does so, its wings,&nbsp;
neck, and head pop up in glorious fashion.

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:13.440
Without going too overboard on details, one&nbsp;
group of Elves that lives in Valinor is called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:16.880
the Teleri. They mostly dwell&nbsp;
in a place called Alqualondë,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:21.520
which loosely translates to "the Haven of&nbsp;
the Swans." "The Silmarillion" explains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:26.720
that the Teleri ships were made to look&nbsp;
like swans with golden beaks and eyes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:32.320
Much later in the book, it explains how the Elves&nbsp;
visited the island nation of Númenor, too, saying,

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:36.720
"And thence at times the&nbsp;
Firstborn still would come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:42.734
sailing to Númenor in oarless boats,&nbsp;
as white birds flying from the sunset."

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:48.240
From the get-go, bird-shaped boats,&nbsp;
and especially swan-shaped boats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:51.358
are a trademark of Elvish maritime activity.

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:56.480
When Galadriel and her company set sail for&nbsp;
Valinor in the first episode of "The Rings of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.480 --> 00:05:04.080
Power," they arrive at a foggy, rainy sea. As the&nbsp;
clouds roll back, it slowly reveals a glorious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:09.200
blinding light, which all of the Elves&nbsp;
but Galadriel willingly enter into.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:14.080
This isn't necessarily how the process of&nbsp;
Elves leaving Middle-earth looks in the books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:19.200
but there's a lot left open to&nbsp;
interpretation. As far as small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:24.640
details are concerned, there's one&nbsp;
specific aspect that is worth calling out.

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:29.920
Twice in "The Lord of the Rings," Frodo&nbsp;
experiences arriving in the Blessed Realm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:34.000
In the books, the first time is in a&nbsp;
dream in "The Fellowship of the Ring."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:37.040
The second time is when he sails&nbsp;
there at the end of "The Return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:41.440
of the King." The book describes&nbsp;
Frodo's experience beautifully:

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:46.560
"And then it seemed to him that as in&nbsp;
his dream in the house of Bombadil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:51.680
the grey rain-curtain turned all to&nbsp;
silver glass and was rolled back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:59.067
and he beheld white shores and beyond them&nbsp;
a far green country under a swift sunrise."

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:03.520
We may not see Valinor in the scene&nbsp;
from "The Rings of Power," but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:08.579
we do see a gray rain curtain, and&nbsp;
it rolls back as they approach.

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:14.160
When we first meet the Harfoots in "The Rings of&nbsp;
Power," they're running circles around a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:20.400
of wary hunters. The Little People lay low and&nbsp;
avoid being seen without any issues. As the Big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:26.640
Folk move along, one of the Harfoots blows a&nbsp;
whistle of sorts to signal the coast is clear.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:32.480
This moment might bring another incident to&nbsp;
mind, a time when Théoden, King of Rohan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:37.920
has a conversation with a couple of Hobbits in&nbsp;
Isengard in "The Two Towers." Merry, Pippin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:43.680
and Théoden all bond over the fact that their&nbsp;
ancestors came from the same area of the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:48.400
an area close to where the Harfoots currently&nbsp;
live when "the Rings of Power" begins.

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:54.240
In the discussion, Théoden mentions that the&nbsp;
Halflings in the apocryphal stories have some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.240 --> 00:07:00.320
interesting skill sets. He adds that they can&nbsp;
vanish in a twinkling. In addition, he says,

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:04.640
"They can change their voices to&nbsp;
resemble the piping of birds."

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:09.200
While that is clearly an exaggeration,&nbsp;
it would appear that the "Rings of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:12.955
Power" writers may be hinting&nbsp;
at how that legend came about.

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:17.120
The Harfoots of "The Rings of Power"&nbsp;
and the Hobbits of later stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:22.560
may be distantly related. But there are&nbsp;
a lot of differences that set them apart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:26.880
not the least of which is the fact&nbsp;
that one group lives a wandering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:32.080
nomadic lifestyle, and the other settle&nbsp;
in undisturbed, domesticated comfort.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:37.280
Even so, some of the throughlines are already&nbsp;
visible, even when the setting is so different.

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:42.800
Harfoots are already shown with a very healthy&nbsp;
appetite for good food. When Nori leads a group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:47.920
of youngsters to a berry patch, their&nbsp;
affinity for food is on full display.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:53.120
Interestingly, the scene also offers a parallel&nbsp;
to the last pages of "The Return of the King."

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:56.800
In that book, Tolkien explains in&nbsp;
the aftermath of the War of the Ring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.800 --> 00:08:00.960
the Shire thrives once Sauron&nbsp;
is defeated. The text says,

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:08.240
"The fruit was so plentiful that young hobbits&nbsp;
very nearly bathed in strawberries and cream;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:13.440
and later they sat on the lawns under the&nbsp;
plum-trees and ate, until they had made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:20.635
piles of stones like small pyramids or the heaped&nbsp;
skulls of a conqueror, and then they moved on."

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:24.400
In the first episode of "The Rings of&nbsp;
Power," there's a point when Marigold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:29.040
Brandyfoot has a serious talk with&nbsp;
Nori about her adventurous behavior.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:34.880
The Halfling mother breaks down how the world&nbsp;
works for the younger, less experienced Nori.

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:43.184
"Elves have forests to protect. Dwarves&nbsp;
their mines. Men their fields of grain."

00:08:43.184 --> 00:08:48.320
She continues that the trees have to worry&nbsp;
about the soil beneath them. But she says,

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:52.240
"We Harfoots are free from the&nbsp;
worries of the wide world."

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:58.480
It leaves Nori squirming but doesn't put a dent&nbsp;
in her desire for adventure. But it also may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:04.560
the first time in the series that Tolkien's Ents&nbsp;
enter the conversation. The Ents are referred to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:10.000
at different times as the Shepherds of the&nbsp;
Trees. In "The Silmarillion," the Ents are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:15.520
explicitly made as a way to guard trees against&nbsp;
other creatures that may want to harm them. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:20.320
presence of Ents in "The Rings of Power" is&nbsp;
established later in the episode. They make a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:26.330
quick cameo when the meteor streaks overhead and&nbsp;
three Ents are shown moving amongst the trees.

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:30.480
When Galadriel returns home&nbsp;
from her long pursuit of Sauron,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:36.320
the High King Gil-galad is convinced to welcome&nbsp;
her as a hero. Despite her rebellious disregard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:43.520
of his orders, the king puts on a celebration to&nbsp;
rival the likes of Bilbo's 111th birthday party.

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:48.320
As the Elves party in Lindon, the&nbsp;
sky lights up with fireworks that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:53.280
take on many different shapes. Some&nbsp;
swirl, while others look like flowers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:58.560
One even takes on the distinct shape&nbsp;
of a butterfly and flaps its wings.

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:02.560
"The Fellowship of the Ring" describes&nbsp;
Gandalf's fireworks at Bilbo's party by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:07.120
saying they included singing bird&nbsp;
sounds, trees losing their leaves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:11.680
eagles, sailing ships, and much&nbsp;
more. It also has the line,

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:15.600
"There were fountains of butterflies&nbsp;
that flew glittering into the trees."

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:21.040
Of course, the question of who's&nbsp;
better at the craft of making fireworks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:24.320
is something that would have to be&nbsp;
solved with a head-to-head contest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:29.801
between the wizard and the Elves. But alas,&nbsp;
it's a competition we'll never get to see.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:34.080
After Galadriel discovers that she's&nbsp;
going to be shipped back home to Valinor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:38.400
she retreats to a peaceful area of the&nbsp;
Lindon forests where Elrond finds her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:43.360
and the two characters proceed to have a&nbsp;
long talk. As they chat, they're surrounded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:49.840
by a string of very Elvish-looking lamps and a&nbsp;
series of statues carved right into living trees.

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:52.880
One of these statues depicts&nbsp;
Galadriel's brother Finrod,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:58.480
who is a First Age hero, but several others&nbsp;
appear as well. We're willing to guess that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.480 --> 00:11:04.880
at least one is the human First Age hero Túrin&nbsp;
Turambar. There are plenty of candidates for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:10.880
who the others could be, but there's one&nbsp;
statue whose identity is without question.

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:15.920
At one point, the camera flashes past a&nbsp;
living wooden statue of a woman with long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:21.440
flowing hair and a shaggy dog in front of her.&nbsp;
There's no doubt that this is an image of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:27.840
immortal maiden Lúthien and the dog Huan. Lúthien&nbsp;
is one of the iconic heroines of Tolkien's world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:33.040
She was inspired by the author's wife&nbsp;
and Lúthien's name was even put on her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:39.440
gravestone. She's known for her hair and also&nbsp;
happens to be Elrond's great-grandmother.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:47.120
The dog, Huan, is also the greatest wolfhound in&nbsp;
Middle-earth history. He talks multiple times and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:49.604
even defeats Sauron in a duel.

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:56.000
When The Stranger crash lands near the Harfoots,&nbsp;
Nori and Poppy try to take care of him. This ends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.000 --> 00:12:01.040
up being more difficult than it seems because&nbsp;
the fellow initially acts like a child.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:04.080
He can't even talk and doesn't&nbsp;
know how to take care of himself.

00:12:04.640 --> 00:12:09.840
For those who know Tolkien, this seems&nbsp;
to point very heavily toward The Stranger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:14.080
being a Wizard. Apart from the fact&nbsp;
that he's supernaturally powerful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:19.520
the whole infantile start to his Middle-earth&nbsp;
tenure points to his Wizarding origins.

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:22.720
In the book "Unfinished Tales,"&nbsp;
Tolkien's son Christopher brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:25.600
together most of his father's&nbsp;
notes about the Wizards or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:29.920
"Istari" as they're called in Elvish.&nbsp;
In that collection of facts, it says,

00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:36.320
"For it is said in deed that being&nbsp;
embodied the Istari had need to learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:42.960
much anew by slow experience, and though they knew&nbsp;
whence they came the memory of the Blessed Realm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:48.080
was to them a vision from afar off,&nbsp;
for which they yearned exceedingly."

00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:52.880
If The Stranger is a Wizard, it would make&nbsp;
sense that after his arrival he would need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:58.880
to relearn some basic things, like talking&nbsp;
and eating. Add onto that the fact that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:05.323
knows a few things, like constellations, who&nbsp;
is The Stranger from "The Rings of Power?"

00:13:05.600 --> 00:13:09.120
As Nori and Poppy wrangle their&nbsp;
mysterious, powerful friend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:13.440
they slowly gain clues about who he&nbsp;
is. By the end of the second episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:18.720
he manages to communicate by using fireflies to&nbsp;
form the shape of a constellation in the sky.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:25.040
Nori gets excited when she realizes that he&nbsp;
wants to find a similar shape of actual stars.

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:29.920
During this scene, there's also a small Easter&nbsp;
egg that calls back to Peter Jackson's films.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:33.360
When Ian McKellen's Gandalf&nbsp;
is trapped on top of Isengard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:38.640
he catches a moth and whispers a message&nbsp;
to it in unintelligible language. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:43.280
Stranger talks to the fireflies in a very&nbsp;
similar manner in "The Rings of Power."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.840 --> 00:13:49.920
This could signal a direct connection between&nbsp;
the characters. Could the Stranger be Gandalf?

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:53.920
It could also simply be a magical&nbsp;
behavior that they're both displaying.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.920 --> 00:13:57.520
This could be meant to showcase&nbsp;
how both characters are Wizards, or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.520 --> 00:14:02.819
at least have a similar spiritual power that&nbsp;
enables them to talk directly to animals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.960 --> 00:14:08.320
Whatever the answer, the way The Stranger chats&nbsp;
up the fireflies looks so similar to Gandalf's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.320 --> 00:14:13.725
discussion with the moth, it's difficult to think&nbsp;
it's anything less than a direct Easter egg.

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:18.320
When Elrond arrives in Eregion in the&nbsp;
second episode of "The Rings of Power,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.320 --> 00:14:24.800
he hits things off with Lord Celebrimbor, whose&nbsp;
craftsmanship he's greatly admired from afar. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:29.280
they get to know one another, they talk about&nbsp;
a crafting hammer on display in Celebrimbor's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.280 --> 00:14:35.440
workshop. It's not just any hammer. It used to&nbsp;
belong to the master Elven craftsman Fëanor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:39.920
While the show makes that fact clear,&nbsp;
the quiet context left out of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:45.360
dialogue is that Celebrimbor likely has&nbsp;
the hammer because he's Fëanor's grandson.

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:48.800
The little details don't stop there.&nbsp;
As the conversation carries on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:54.960
the two Elves look at plans to build a giant,&nbsp;
overheated new addition to Celebrimbor's workshop.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.960 --> 00:15:01.520
The Elven master craftsman says that he wants to&nbsp;
build a tower that could hold the most powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.520 --> 00:15:07.920
forge ever constructed in Middle-earth. One&nbsp;
that would burn as hot as a dragon's tongue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:13.200
The subtle reference brings to mind a line from&nbsp;
"The Fellowship of the Ring" where Gandalf says,

00:15:13.760 --> 00:15:18.880
"It has been said that dragon-fire could&nbsp;
melt and consume the Rings of Power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.520 --> 00:15:25.760
but there is not now any dragon left on&nbsp;
earth in which the old fire is hot enough."

00:15:25.760 --> 00:15:30.560
The reference to a forge as hot as a&nbsp;
dragon's tongue is a fitting description,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.560 --> 00:15:36.395
because chances are, Celebrimbor has plans to&nbsp;
use his new forge to create the Rings of Power.

00:15:36.640 --> 00:15:41.520
When Elrond and Celebrimbor head over to&nbsp;
Khazad-dûm to recruit the help of the Dwarves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:47.200
the Lord of Eregion informs his companion&nbsp;
that he's always admired the Dwarves and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.200 --> 00:15:52.960
wonders if he'll be able to see them at work&nbsp;
in their forges. Elrond sets some rather lofty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.960 --> 00:15:58.240
expectations in response, pointing out that&nbsp;
he thinks they'll do a lot more than that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:03.440
He launches into a description of Dwarven&nbsp;
hospitality that includes blaring Ram's horns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.440 --> 00:16:08.560
tables full of salted pork,&nbsp;
and lots and lots of malt beer.

00:16:08.560 --> 00:16:13.680
If the depiction sounds familiar, it's because&nbsp;
Gimli talks about the same kind of Dwarven welcome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.680 --> 00:16:18.640
thousands of years later during "The Lord of the&nbsp;
Rings." In "The Fellowship of the Ring," as Frodo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.640 --> 00:16:24.720
and company enter Moria, Gimli warns Legolas to&nbsp;
prepare for the fabled hospitality of the Dwarves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.720 --> 00:16:27.840
adding what the Elf should&nbsp;
expect to find waiting for him.

00:16:28.480 --> 00:16:34.240
"Roaring fires, malt beer,&nbsp;
ripe meat off the bone."

00:16:34.240 --> 00:16:37.440
Tying these references together&nbsp;
creates some continuity between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.440 --> 00:16:41.840
the two distinctly different versions&nbsp;
of Middle-earth. Both may be their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.840 --> 00:16:47.290
adaptations, but when it comes to Dwarven&nbsp;
hospitality, everyone's on the same page.

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:53.440
The Dwarves have an interesting creation story.&nbsp;
Unlike Men and Elves who are created by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.440 --> 00:16:59.440
God-like supreme being Ilúvatar, Dwarves&nbsp;
are initially formed by the angelic being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.440 --> 00:17:04.880
Aulë. Though he makes them, Aulë can't&nbsp;
give his Dwarves sentient life. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.880 --> 00:17:10.080
something that only Ilúvatar can do, and&nbsp;
he eventually gets around to it. Even so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.080 --> 00:17:15.840
the Dwarves always have a special affinity for&nbsp;
Aulë, and it shows in "The Rings of Power."

00:17:15.840 --> 00:17:21.760
In the rock-breaking scene, Durin IV references&nbsp;
his semi-maker when he describes the event&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.760 --> 00:17:26.800
as "The Dwarven test of endurance&nbsp;
fashioned by Aulë himself." Later on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.800 --> 00:17:33.200
when Durin and Elrond are arguing, Disa expresses&nbsp;
her disapproval by referencing Aulë's beard.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.200 --> 00:17:39.600
For most viewers, it's a random and odd name.&nbsp;
For Tolkien fans, it's a clear and well-placed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:44.000
reference to the greatest being in&nbsp;
all of Dwarven culture and history.

00:17:44.000 --> 00:17:48.400
It's also worth pointing out that the full&nbsp;
Dwarven face masks worn by many of the guards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.400 --> 00:17:52.640
of Khazad-dûm get their inspiration&nbsp;
from "The Silmarillion." At one point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.640 --> 00:17:55.680
the Dwarves take on a bunch of dragons, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.680 --> 00:18:01.360
they can do because of their hideous but nearly&nbsp;
indestructible masks. It's a one-off reference,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.360 --> 00:18:07.299
and it's fun to see the famous headgear&nbsp;
translated into the garb of Durin's guards.

00:18:07.600 --> 00:18:12.240
When we first meet Arondir in the Southlands&nbsp;
of Middle-earth, he's visiting a town of Men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:17.200
with a fellow Elven warden. While there,&nbsp;
he interacts with the Human woman Bronwyn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:17.200 --> 00:18:24.160
and it's clear that the two are in love. As they&nbsp;
leave, Arondir and his fellow Elf verbally spar.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.160 --> 00:18:28.320
Eventually, the talk turns more&nbsp;
serious, and Arondir's companion says,

00:18:28.320 --> 00:18:34.080
"My point is this. Only twice in known&nbsp;
history has a pairing between Elves and Humans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.080 --> 00:18:40.320
even been attempted. And on each occasion,&nbsp;
it ended in tragedy. It ended in death."

00:18:40.320 --> 00:18:45.920
The reference is to two distinct pairings. The&nbsp;
first is the mortal man Beren and the immortal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.920 --> 00:18:51.920
Lúthien. They get hitched and end up dying&nbsp;
... twice. The other pair is the Man Tuor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.920 --> 00:18:55.920
and the Elf-maiden Idril. While&nbsp;
they have a happier ending,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:55.920 --> 00:19:00.680
their tale is also filled with&nbsp;
some epic and tragic events.

